 "literal": "Not obtaining fears; if obtained, separation fears; unending affliction-natured is my heart.",
 "translation": "My heart is possessed of unending affliction: it fears when it does not obtain union, and if it does obtain it, it fears separation.",
 "modern": "என் நெஞ்சம் எப்போதும் நீங்காத துன்பத்தை உடையதாக இருக்கிறது; காதலரைப் பெறாதபோது பெறவில்லையே என்று அஞ்சுகிறது, பெற்றுவிட்டால் பிரிந்துவிடுவாரோ என்று அஞ்சுகிறது.",
 "explanation": "This couplet portrays the constant state of anxiety experienced by a lover's heart. When the lover is absent, the heart is filled with fear that it will never achieve union. However, even when union is achieved, the heart cannot rejoice fully because it immediately begins to fear the pain of future separation. Through the words 'peṟāamai añcum' (fears not obtaining) and 'peṟiṉ pirivu añcum' (if obtained, fears separation), the poet shows how the heart is trapped in a loop of worry, making its sorrow truly 'aṟāa iṭumpai' (ceaseless affliction).",
